GitHub Actions – Modern CI/CD (Production Ready)

For 2–4 years experience, interviewers expect you to design secure, optimized, and scalable CI/CD pipelines, not just write a basic YAML file.

🔹 GitHub Actions Interview Questions (2–4 yrs)

• What is GitHub Actions and why is it popular?
• How does GitHub Actions work internally?
• Workflow vs Job vs Step 
• What are runners? (Hosted vs Self-hosted)
• How does GitHub Actions differ from Jenkins?
• What triggers can start a workflow?
• How do you control pipeline execution using filters?
• What are matrix builds and their use cases?
• How do you manage secrets securely in GitHub Actions?
• What are GitHub environments?
• How do approvals work before deployment?
• How do you reuse workflows?
• How do you optimize pipeline execution time?
• GitHub Actions with Docker
• GitHub Actions with Kubernetes
• How do you implement CI + CD using GitHub Actions?
• How do you secure GitHub Actions pipelines?

🔹 Real-World GitHub Actions Scenarios

• Pipeline running on every commit — how do you restrict it?
• Secrets exposed in workflow logs — how will you fix it?
• Workflow passes in feature branch but fails in main — why?
• Deployment should happen only after manual approval — how?
• How do you rollback a deployment using GitHub Actions?
• How do you manage multi-environment deployments (Dev / QA / Prod)?

🔹 Best Practices (2–4 yrs Level)

• Use environment protection rules
• Use reusable workflows
• Separate CI and CD pipelines
• Store secrets in GitHub Secrets (never in YAML)
• Use self-hosted runners for better control
• Enable branch protection rules
• Follow least-privilege access

💡 Interview Tip:
If you say:
 “We use GitHub Actions with environment approvals, reusable workflows, and branch protection rules”
you already sound like a strong 2–4 years DevOps engineer.
